From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rui Figueira <rfigueira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 16:09:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Compute an infinite or NaN hue as 0 degrees
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

css-color-4 treats an infinite or NaN <hue> as 0deg. WPT applies this
to the computed value, and expects the specified value to keep the
calc() result as written. normalize_hue() computed
hue - 360 * floor(hue / 360), which is NaN for any non-finite hue, so:

- lch(50 10 calc(infinity)) computed to lch(50 10 calc(NaN)), not
  lch(50 10 0).
- Its specified value serialized as lch(50 10 calc(NaN)), not
  lch(50 10 calc(infinity)).

Parsing now keeps a non-finite hue as it is, and
AbsoluteColor::zero_non_finite_hue() sets it to 0 when the color is
computed or resolved at use time. Color space conversions also treat a
non-finite hue as 0. This applies to hsl(), hwb(), lch() and oklch().

Gecko runs this same code, so Firefox has the same bug.

WPT: css/css-color/parsing/color-computed-lab.html and
color-valid-lab.html.

beep-boop-🤖
